Advanced/Complicated COPD
Respiratory System
Barrel chest, Pursed-lip breathing, Cachexia, Irreversible airflow limitation

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: RPS-003
Reason for Visit: Acute worsening of chronic dyspnea and increased work of breathing requiring urgent evaluation to prevent decompensation into acute respiratory failure.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and adult child
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated severe, progressive dyspnea at rest and increased use of accessory muscles. Urgent assessment was necessary to intervene before hypoxemia worsened, avoiding delayed care until the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I cannot catch my breath even when I am just sitting here, and I feel completely drained.
History of Present Illness: Patient reports a steady decline in respiratory reserve over the last 48 hours. The severe, progressive dyspnea has resulted in severe exercise limitation, leaving the patient bed-to-chair bound. The patient reports an inability to clear secretions effectively. Unintended weight loss and cachexia have been noted over the past several months. The patient relies on pursed-lip breathing to maintain airway patency but is fatiguing.
Functional Status: Highly limited; requires assistance for activities of daily living.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk for falls due to weakness; caregiver burden noted.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 145/90 mmHg | HR: 110 bpm | Temp: 36.8 C | RR: 28 breaths/min | SpO2: 88% on room air | Wt: 55 Kg
General: Cachectic appearance, sitting in a tripod position, visibly utilizing pursed-lip breathing, central cyanosis noted at the lips.
Physical Exam: Chest examination reveals a pronounced barrel chest deformity. Auscultation demonstrates significantly decreased breath sounds globally and hyperresonance to percussion bilaterally. The patient exhibits use of accessory muscles of respiration at rest. Signs of right heart failure including mild peripheral edema are present. Irreversible airflow limitation is presumed based on the clinical picture and history.
Environmental Observations: Oxygen tubing presents a trip hazard; home environment is cluttered.
Medication Reconciliation: Using long-acting muscarinic antagonists and inhaled corticosteroids; adherence is reportedly good.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Advanced/Complicated COPD - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Congestive Heart Failure - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of elevated JVP, bibasilar crackles, and presence of hyperinflated lungs.

Pneumothorax - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Bilateral symmetrical decrease in breath sounds rather than acute unilateral absence.

Lung Malignancy - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Acute nature of the current exacerbation responding to initial bronchodilation, though underlying risk remains.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient demonstrates advanced COPD with an acute exacerbation, evidenced by severe dyspnea, pursed-lip breathing, accessory muscle use, and borderline hypoxemia. The cachectic state and right heart failure signs indicate minimal physiological reserve. An urgent after-hours home visit was absolutely necessary to assess the degree of respiratory failure, titrate supplemental oxygen cautiously, and administer systemic corticosteroids and bronchodilators. Delaying this assessment until the next in-hours period would predictably result in acute hypercapnic respiratory failure requiring intubation and ICU admission.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered oral prednisolone 50mg and nebulized bronchodilators. Titrated home oxygen via nasal prongs to target SpO2 88-92%.
Positive Behavioral Change: Keep the oxygen tubing routed along the baseboards rather than across the floor; doing this removes the tripping hazard, which directly prevents falls and subsequent hospitalizations.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Urgent referral to community respiratory nursing team.
DME/Safety (Optional): Four-wheel walker recommended for indoor mobility.
Follow-up: GP review tomorrow; continuous monitoring required.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate transfer via ambulance (000) to the Emergency Department if the patient becomes confused, drowsy, or if breathing becomes severely labored despite treatment.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis reflects an acute exacerbation of a chronic irreversible condition; formal spirometry confirms irreversible airflow limitation.
Clinical Course: The exacerbation requires several days of intensive medical therapy to return the patient to their baseline, which is already significantly compromised.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Call 000 immediately if you feel confused, if your fingers or lips turn blue despite your oxygen, or if you cannot speak at all.
PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Steroids reduce the severe swelling in the lung tubes, and targeted oxygen therapy provides enough oxygen without stopping the natural drive to breathe.
Actionable Steps: Sit leaning forward on a table with pillows; this specific posture takes the weight off your chest muscles, allowing your diaphragm to work more efficiently and reducing breathing effort.

